Review of #54 at `2542d99`, comment only. I read the whole diff, and ran it merged with #51, #52, #53 and #55 on top of main `9f78440` (reference VM `6.18.0-vpt1-ref`, x86-64, a long-lived working copy, not the AN1 master; Rust on Linux in WSL). Not run alone.

**Results:** Linux: `clippy -D warnings` 0, `cargo test --locked` all pass (110 library tests, 27 adversarial). VM: `rj4_reload.py` **31/31**, `ay2_controller` 23/23, `ay4_usage_rekey` 7/7, `ay4_soak` 9/9, `demo_reachability` 34/34, 0 build warnings, module unloads with no leaks, no kernel faults. Hosted CI is green.

**What I like:** the controller follows the kernel's own classification (the peer incarnation in STATUS) instead of repeating it, so it cannot disagree with `peer_cfg_equal`; the file is read bounded and compared by content; a refusal leaves the running configuration untouched; and a policy-only reload sends no INIT and loses no pings in your test.

**Concerns:**
1. **Transient kernel errors are treated as permanent.** `RELOAD_REFUSALS` includes `EBUSY` and `ENOMEM`, and a refused version is not retried until the file changes. In the kernel, `EBUSY` from `COMMIT_CONFIG` means the retired-snapshot ceiling (two snapshots waiting for RCU reclamation; `gateway.c`), which clears by itself after a grace period, and `ENOMEM` is also transient. A reload that hits either is dropped with only a log line, and the running configuration then silently differs from the file until the next edit. `EINVAL`, `ENOSPC`, `E2BIG` and `EMSGSIZE` are genuinely permanent for that content. Please retry `EBUSY` and `ENOMEM` after a short delay (a few seconds, a few attempts). I could not reproduce `EBUSY` from userspace (it needs readers holding old snapshots), so this is from the kernel code, not observed.
2. **Code conflict with #51 in `daemon.rs`** (not only docs). #51 takes every `now` from `BootClock`; this PR adds `Instant::now()` in `Controller::new` is unchanged but in the new `reload()` and in the loop. After merging both, the reload path must also use `clock.now()`, or deadlines after a reload would be on a different clock from the rest. I resolved it by hand (pass `clock.now()` into `reload()`, then to `reconfigure()`), and the merged tree built and passed everything above. Whichever merges second needs that rebase.
3. `docs/CONTROLLER.md` and `tools/kernel_vm_suite.sh` also conflict with #52 and #55 (adjacent additions only).

Not run: the rest of the suite on this head alone, a multi-peer reload on the VM (removal of one of several peers is unit-tested only), ARM64, a Pi. I am not approving; merging is for Aayush to decide.

Clarification of item 2 in my review above, which was worded badly. After #51 merges, `daemon.rs` takes every time value from `BootClock` (`clock.now()`). This PR's new `reload()` calls `Instant::now()` itself when it hands the time to `Controller::reconfigure()`, and its loop still uses `Instant::now()`. Whichever PR merges second has to switch those to `clock.now()`: pass the time into `reload()` as a parameter. I did exactly that in my test merge, and it built and passed.

